# The OCR sandbox (ADR-0028). Its own policy, deliberately different from the
# application's above — and the only place `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` is permitted.
#
# The application origin must never have it: that origin holds the GitHub token and
# the BYOK AI key. This document holds nothing. It is embedded with
# `sandbox="allow-scripts"` and no `allow-same-origin`, so it runs on an opaque origin
# with no access to the app's storage, and `connect-src 'self'` lets it load its own
# model files and reach nothing else.
#
# The two `!` lines are the load-bearing part, and they are there because the previous
# fix was built on a wrong idea of how this file works.
#
# **Cloudflare appends; it does not override.** `/*` matches `/ocr/*` too, and naming the
# same header in this block does not replace the inherited value — both are sent.
# Verified 2026-09-15 by serving the real build through `wrangler dev`: every `/ocr/*`
# response carried *two* `Content-Security-Policy` headers and *two* `X-Frame-Options`
# values. The earlier belief — that a block overrides the headers it names — is why
# simply setting `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN` here did not work.
#
# Two CSPs are enforced cumulatively: a resource must satisfy every policy, so the
# application's `script-src 'self'` intersected with this one and removed
# `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` again, while `frame-ancestors 'none'` intersected to forbid
# framing outright. Two `X-Frame-Options` values are a conflict, which Chromium resolves
# as DENY. Either alone is enough to make the sandbox unreachable, and the symptom is an
# empty frame — which reads exactly like "WebAssembly does not work in an opaque origin".
#
# `! Content-Security-Policy` and `! X-Frame-Options` drop the inherited values so the
# ones below are the only ones sent. `scripts/check-csp.mjs` now fails the build if
# either is missing.
#
# `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin` — and it cannot be anything stricter.
# This document runs on an **opaque origin**, which belongs to no site and no origin, so
# `same-site` and `same-origin` can never match it: the frame loads and is then refused
# every one of its own assets (`NS_ERROR_DOM_CORP_FAILED` on `sandbox.js`). `cross-origin`
# is the only value an opaque-origin document can satisfy.
#
# What that costs: these files become readable by any origin. They are the OCR engine
# and its models — public static assets with nothing user-specific in them — so the cost
# is hotlinking, not disclosure. Framing the sandbox *document* is still restricted by
# `frame-ancestors 'self'` above, and that is the control that matters.
/ocr/*
  ! Content-Security-Policy
  ! X-Frame-Options
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; connect-src 'self'; img-src blob: data:; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; frame-ancestors 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
